  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Data view: Kibana Sample Data Logs** (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`)\n- Request fields: `url`, `request`, `response` (status code), `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent`, plus geo fields (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`) and `machine.os`.\n- Each field has a `.keyword` subfield for exact matching.\n\n**Dashboard: EFF-202013d0 Web logs overview**\n- URL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-202013d0-web-logs-overview-57a819469346\n- Six panels, default time range last 1 year:\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n  - Average response size (average of `bytes`)\n  - Requests over time (area, date histogram)\n  - Responses by status code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n  - Top requested URLs (table, terms on `url.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule: EFF-202013d0 5xx spike**\n- Rule ID `aa21d8e9-9026-4b95-adbb-f21f58cd7153`, type `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`, tagged `EFF-202013d0`.\n- Runs every 1m. Enabled.\n- Query: `response.keyword` matches wildcard `5??` (any 3-character code starting with 5).\n- Fires when the count over the last 5 minutes is greater than 10.\n\n**Things I could not verify**\n- I could not read the actual status-code values. The ES|QL, console proxy, and internal search endpoints all returned 404 or 400, and Elasticsearch is not reachable on localhost:9200. I chose the `5??` wildcard based on the mapping and the usual web-log format. The pie panel will show the real distribution. If the data holds no 5xx codes, the rule will never fire.\n- The rule's status shows `pending`, which is expected right after creation. I did not wait for a run.",
